<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thiès</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Thies</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thiès is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, about seventy kilometres east of Dakar and one of the country's largest cities. It grew as a junction of the Dakar–Saint-Louis railway and the Dakar–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> line, with major railway workshops, and was the centre of the great railway strike of 1947–1948 in French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Thiès lies 72 km east of Dakar on the N2 road, at the junction of railway lines to Dakar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a> and Saint-Louis. It is the capital of Thiès Region. Before colonisation the Thiès Plateau was a wooded frontier between the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baol">Baol</a>, inhabited by the Serer-Noon, a sub-group of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> people, who speak the Noon language and still live in the Thiès-Nones quarter of the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The village of Dianxene, belonging to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a>, was founded on the plateau in the 17th century and had only 75 inhabitants in 1860. The French founded a military post there in 1864, and the Spiritans later set up a mission. In 1885 the Dakar–Saint-Louis railway, the first line in French West Africa, opened with a stop at Thiès. The Dakar–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> line, built between 1906 and 1923, made it a junction. The French created the commune of Thiès in 1904.</p><p>The railways brought commerce and migrant labourers, including Bambara from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. The rail workers played a key part in the rise of Senegal's labour movement. A railway strike was violently repressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the city was a centre of the general strikes of 1945–1946 and 1947–1948, which also marked the growth of the independence movement across French West Africa. Léopold Sédar Senghor, later the first president of independent Senegal, was elected mayor in November 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Thiès is the hub of an agricultural area producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peanut">peanuts</a>, manioc, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and fruit, and a centre of livestock trading and meat packing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phosphate">Phosphate</a> is worked nearby at Palo Dial and Taïba. A tapestry factory was set up in 1966 to produce work designed by Senegalese artists, and the city has the University of Thiès, a museum and a crafts village. It is twinned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Sousse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solingen">Solingen</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thiès” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
